---
description: State ownership and derivation
globs: "**/*.{ts,tsx}"
alwaysApply: true
---

- Keep application state and business logic in RxJS state management; UI components render and handle interaction.
- Keep transient, rapidly changing UI state local to the component that renders it.
- Do not pass subscriptions or rapidly changing values through intermediate components.
- Put shared logic in the appropriate common package; split by platform only when behavior differs.
- Derive state from props, context, or actual observables, never from hardcoded defaults. If a default is necessary, use a named constant and document why it is safe.
- Sizing, clamping, and related effects must use the same observables so they cannot fight. When stored and clamped values coexist, derive behavior from the source representing current user intent.

Extract a hook when logic represents independent behavior, including interaction handling, subscriptions, state transitions, async workflows, or reusable derived state. Components describe rendering; hooks own behavior.
